#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# plot

#% text
# `plot(f, [x, a, b])` draws the curve of `f` for `x` from `a` to `b` as an image.

#% text
# `plot([f1, f2, ...], [x, a, b])` draws several curves in one picture, each in its own colour.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The value is `image("path")`, a PNG file; the notebook and this documentation show it in place.

#% item
# The function is sampled adaptively: evenly at first, then more densely where the curve bends.

#% item
# Where `f` is not a finite real number, jumps by more than the visible range (at a pole), or still jumps after the sampling has been refined as far as it goes (a step such as `floor(x)`), the curve is broken.

#% item
# The vertical range is chosen so that a few extreme values do not flatten the rest of the curve.

#% item
# Options:

#% subitem
# `x_label: "text"` and `y_label: "text"` label the axes.

#% subitem
# `legend: ["text", ...]` names the curves; `legend_position: top_right | top_left | bottom_left | bottom_right` places the box.

#% subitem
# `colors: ["#rrggbb", ...]` and `line_style: [solid | dashed | dotted | dot_dashed, ...]` set the look of each curve.

#% subitem
# `x_scale` and `y_scale`: `linear`, `log`, or `signed_log`.

#% subitem
# `plot_range: [y0, y1]` or `[[x0, x1], [y0, y1]]` fixes the range shown (an end may be `automatic`); the curves are cut off at its edges.

#% subitem
# `filling: axis | bottom | top | c | [i, j]` fills, faintly in each curve's colour, from the curve to the x axis, the bottom or top of the plot or the line y = c, or between curves i and j.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# One period of the sine:

#% cell
plot(sin(x), [x, 0, 2pi])
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-1.png")

#% text
# Two curves together:

#% cell
plot([sin(x), cos(x)], [x, 0, 2pi])
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-2.png")

#% text
# A polynomial with its three real roots:

#% cell
plot(x^3 - 3x, [x, -2.5, 2.5])
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-3.png")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Poles break the curve instead of joining its branches:

#% cell
plot(tan(x), [x, -3, 3])
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-4.png")

#% text
# A function with a rapid oscillation near zero; sampling refines there:

#% cell
plot(x*sin(1/x), [x, -0.5, 0.5])
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-5.png")

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# Labels and a legend:

#% cell
plot([exp(-x), exp(-2x)], [x, 0, 3], x_label: "t", y_label: "amount", legend: ["rate 1", "rate 2"])
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-6.png")

#% text
# A logarithmic vertical axis turns exponentials into straight lines:

#% cell
plot([exp(x), exp(2x)], [x, 0, 5], y_scale: log)
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-7.png")

#% text
# Colours and line styles:

#% cell
plot([sin(x), sin(2x)], [x, 0, pi], colors: ["#e45649", "#4078f2"], line_style: [solid, dashed])
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-8.png")

#% text
# The region between the curve and the axis filled, and the vertical range chosen:

#% cell
plot(sin(x)/x, [x, -15, 15], filling: axis, plot_range: [-0.4, 1.1])
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-9.png")

#% text
# The region between two curves filled:

#% cell
plot([1 + x/2, exp(x/2)], [x, 0, 3], filling: [1, 2])
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-10.png")

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A function and its derivative:

#% cell
plot([x*exp(-x), d(x*exp(-x), x)], [x, 0, 6], legend: ["f", "f'"])
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-11.png")

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The solutions of an equation, as rules, give one curve each:

#% cell
plot(t*x /. solve(x^2 == 4, x), [t, 0, 1])
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-12.png")

#% text
# A solution of a differential equation, named in the legend:

#% cell
plot(y(t) /. n_dsolve([y'(t) == -y(t)/2, y(0) == 1], y, [t, 0, 6]), [t, 0, 6], plot_legends: automatic)
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-13.png")

#% text
# A quantity: its unit labels the axis:

#% cell
plot(quantity(9.81*t^2/2, "m"), [t, quantity(0, "s"), quantity(3, "s")])
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-14.png")

#% text
# `legended`, `labeled` and `style` around a curve give its legend entry, its label and its style:

#% cell
plot([legended(sin(x), "sine"), style(cos(x), red, dashed)], [x, 0, 2*pi])
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-15.png")

#% text
# A density of a distribution:

#% cell
plot(pdf(normal_distribution(0, 1), x), [x, -3, 3])
#% output
#= image("plot.files/plot-16.png")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `list_plot`, `table`, `n_dsolve`
